        "problem": "$(3+x)^{5}=$",
        "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\frac{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+\u22ef+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(3+x)^{5},$ we have  $a=3,$ $b=x,$ $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(3+x)^{5}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0}){(3)}^{5}{(x)}^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1}){(3)}^{5-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2}){(3)}^{5-2}{(x)}^{2}+\u2026 \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{5}+5{(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{1}+10{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{2}+\u2026 \\\\  & =243+405x+270{x}^{2}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
